## Not one piece at a time

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 writes text one token at a time (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />). Many image generators work differently: they
start with the whole picture as random noise and clean it up a little at a time, everywhere at
once. This is a <GlossaryPopover term="diffusion">diffusion model</GlossaryPopover>.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 writes text one token at a time, left to right (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />). A picture could be made the same
way, one patch after another, but many image generators work differently: they start with the whole
picture as random noise and clean it up a little at a time, everywhere at once. This is a <GlossaryPopover term="diffusion">diffusion model</GlossaryPopover>.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

Training works backwards. Take a real picture and add noise, bit by bit, until nothing of it is
left. A network learns to look at a noisy picture and guess the noise that was added.<Cite id="sohl-dickstein2015" />
To make something new, start from pure noise and keep removing the noise it predicts.<Cite id="ho2020" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Training works backwards from the result. Take a real picture and add a little random noise, then a
little more, step by step, until nothing of the picture is left. A network is trained to look at a
noisy picture, told how many steps of noise it has had, and guess the noise that was
added.<Cite id="sohl-dickstein2015" /> To make something new, start from pure noise and repeatedly
subtract the noise the network predicts. Each step leaves something a little more like the training
pictures.<Cite id="ho2020" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The noise, written down

The toy adds noise in 50 steps, each a little bigger than the last: the share $\beta_t$ grows from
0.001 to 0.2. You don't have to add it step by step. With $\bar\alpha_t$ the product of all the
$(1 - \beta)$s up to step $t$, the point after $t$ steps is

$$x_t = \sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\,x_0 + \sqrt{1 - \bar\alpha_t}\,\varepsilon$$

where $x_0$ is the clean point and $\varepsilon$ is fresh random noise.<Cite id="ho2020" /> After all
50 steps $\bar\alpha$ is about 0.0045, so less than 7% of the original point is left. Training picks
a random step, makes $x_t$ this way, and scores the network by the squared error between its guess
and $\varepsilon$.

</At>

## Try it with dots

<At levels="simple">

Here, each "picture" is a single dot, and the training pictures are dots scattered along a shape.
Train the tiny network to predict the noise, then paint 400 new dots, each starting from pure
noise:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A real image has millions of numbers. Here, each "picture" is a single dot with just two numbers,
its position, and the training pictures are dots scattered along a shape. Train the tiny network
(two layers of neurons like the ones in <ChapterRef to="a-neuron" />) to predict the noise, then paint 400 new dots,
each starting from pure noise:

</At>

<DiffusionToy />

Each dot is drawn independently, from its own random start, yet together they land on the shape.
The network never stored the training dots; it learned which way to nudge a noisy dot to make it
more like them. Before training, the nudges are random, and painting gives a smudge.

<At levels="deep">

### One step back

To paint, each step takes the network's guess $\hat\varepsilon$ and removes the share of it that
this step would have added, then adds a little fresh noise $\sigma_t z$, none at the very last
step:

$$x_{t-1} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - \beta_t}}\left(x_t - \frac{\beta_t}{\sqrt{1 - \bar\alpha_t}}\,\hat\varepsilon\right) + \sigma_t z$$

This is the sampling rule from the paper the toy follows.<Cite id="ho2020" /> Run 50 times, from
step 50 down to 1, it turns a cloud of random dots into the shape.

</At>

## From dots to pictures

<At levels="simple">

Real image generators do the same with far bigger networks and every pixel's colors. Many work on
a compressed version of the image and steer each step with your text description.<Cite id="rombach2022" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Real image generators do the same thing with far bigger networks and with every pixel's colors
instead of two numbers. To save work, *latent* diffusion models remove the noise from a compressed
version of the image, and they can steer each step with a text description, so the noise becomes
the picture you asked for.<Cite id="rombach2022" /> The original recipe took 1,000 small steps to
make one picture;<Cite id="ho2020" /> the toy above uses 50.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A diffusion model learns to predict the noise that was added to a training example.
- To make something new, it starts from pure noise and removes a little predicted noise at each
  step.
- Unlike GPT-2's one-token-at-a-time writing, it refines the whole picture at every step.

<At levels="deep">

- $x_t = \sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\,x_0 + \sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\,\varepsilon$ gives any step's noise in one go; painting
  removes it one step at a time.

</At>
</RecapCard>

